R=Path(__file__).resolve().parent;P=R.parent;paper=P/'manuscript/paper'
s=json.loads((R/'summary.json').read_text());g=s['groups'];audit=json.loads((R/'audit.json').read_text());ex=json.loads((R/'execution_audit.json').read_text());diag=json.loads((R/'timing_sensitivity/summary.json').read_text())
assert audit['all_sources_match'] and audit['all_caps_pass'] and diag['selected_unchanged']
labels={'coarse8':'Coarse 8','coarse32':'Coarse 32','phys8':'Corrected 8','phys32':'Corrected 32','hf_online':'HF online','hf32':'HF reuse 32','hf128':'HF reuse 128'}
def pct(a,metric='test',cap=180):return 100*g[f'{cap}_{a}'][metric+'_mean']
def pair(a,b):return next(x for x in s['paired'] if x['budget']==180 and x['metric']=='test' and x['left']==a and x['right']==b)
rows=list(csv.DictReader((R/'all_runs.csv').open()))
cs=[]
for a in labels:
 rr=[r for r in rows if r['arm']==a and r['budget']=='180']
 cs.append({'arm':a,'label_seconds':statistics.mean(sum(float(r[k+'_seconds']) for k in ['hf','coarse8','coarse32','phys8','phys32']) for r in rr),'training_seconds':g['180_'+a]['training_seconds_mean'],'steps':g['180_'+a]['steps_mean'],'ready':g['180_'+a]['decision_ready_mean']})
(R/'cost_summary.json').write_text(json.dumps(cs,indent=2))
text=[r'\subsection{Interpretation, actual costs, and execution audit}',f"HF reuse 128 has the lowest mean ID and OOD errors among the seven selected configurations: {pct('hf128'):.3f}\\% and {pct('hf128','ood'):.3f}\\%. Against online acquisition, its ID advantage is {-pair('hf128','hf_online')['mean_difference_pp']:.3f} percentage points, with all five pairs favoring reuse. This does not imply that any small labeled set is sufficient: reuse 32 is worse than online acquisition in all five ID pairs.",f"The correction results are mixed and remain in the main report. Corrected 8 improves markedly over coarse 8, but corrected 32 has higher ID error than coarse 32 in all five pairs (mean difference {pair('phys32','coarse32')['mean_difference_pp']:.3f} percentage points). Corrected 32 also loses to HF reuse 128 in all five pairs. Thus the conclusion depends on the strength of the HF-reuse comparator. The cost ledger is consistent with an optimization-opportunity trade-off, but does not isolate its causal effect: learning rates differ after equal-budget selection, and update schedules and targets differ.",r'The architecture and optimizer are the same FNO and AdamW used above. Nominal batches contain eight draws with duplicate indices removed; the learning rate decays as $\eta_0/(1+t/60)$. Each run reserves 12 seconds near budget boundaries for validation and checkpoint handling. Caps are not exact expenditures: mean final decision-ready times across arms range from '+f"{min(x['ready'] for x in cs):.2f} to {max(x['ready'] for x in cs):.2f} seconds. Initialization, input construction, validation, and remaining control overhead stay in wall time.",r'\begin{table}[ht]\centering\small',r'\caption{Dependent 120-second endpoints and costs at the 180-second cap. Errors are percentages; all entries are means over five training units. Label time includes HF, coarse, and correction computation. Other overhead is included in wall time.}',r'\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}\toprule Method & ID$_{120}$ & OOD$_{120}$ & Labels (s) & Train (s) & Updates\\\midrule']
for c in cs:
 a=c['arm'];text.append(f"{labels[a]} & {pct(a,cap=120):.3f} & {pct(a,'ood',120):.3f} & {c['label_seconds']:.1f} & {c['training_seconds']:.1f} & {c['steps']:.1f}\\\\")
text.extend([r'\bottomrule\end{tabular}\end{table}',f"All 21 primary tuning trials and 35 formal trajectories satisfy their caps, with no non-finite loss flags; all frozen source hashes match. Maximum observed HF residual is {audit['max_hf_residual']:.3g}, with at most {audit['max_hf_iterations']} CG iterations. The shared reference dataset and post-run testing are separate expenses.",f"Five-second monitoring detected competing Python load in {ex['observed_affected_tuning']} primary tuning trial(s) and {ex['observed_affected_formal']} formal trajectories; {ex['unmonitored_runs']} early tuning trial lacked monitoring. This is not a guarantee of complete host isolation. Two diagnostics, chosen solely from these execution flags, repeat the affected or unmonitored tuning cases under idle conditions. They add 120 seconds of allocated diagnostic budget, are separate from the equal 180-second primary tuning allowance per arm, do not evaluate test sets, and do not change the selected configurations or formal models."])
for d in diag['results']:
 text.append(f"For {labels[d['arm']]} at learning rate {d['lr']}, original/repeated validation errors are {100*d['original_validation']:.3f}\\%/{100*d['repeat_validation']:.3f}\\%, with {d['original_steps']}/{d['repeat_steps']} updates. The repeated validation value {'remains' if d['repeat_remains_below_other_original_candidates'] else 'does not remain'} below the other original candidates.")
text.append(r'Hyperparameter-selection uncertainty is not replicated: one tuning pool and a 32-input validation set select configurations. Five training units and a shared evaluation set support a bounded empirical comparison, not a universal ranking or a precise small-effect claim. The approximate paired intervals are exploratory, and the exact two-sided sign test has minimum $p=0.0625$.')
(paper/'formal_interpretation.tex').write_text('\n\n'.join(text)+'\n')
m=(paper/'main.tex').read_text();m=m.replace('\\date{26 October 2026}','\\date{27 October 2026}')
a=m.index('\\noindent\\textbf{Status.}');b=m.index('\\begin{abstract}',a)
m=m[:a]+r'\noindent\textbf{Status.} Research draft with a completed prospective, replicated strong-solver comparison. Claims remain limited to one PDE family, one architecture, and the stated CPU construction budgets. Author declarations and a public release remain outstanding.'+'\n'+m[b:]
a=m.index('\\begin{abstract}');b=m.index('\\end{abstract}',a)+len('\\end{abstract}')
abstract=r'''\begin{abstract}
Fewer high-fidelity labels need not imply a better neural operator under a fixed construction budget. We compare measured expenditure on numerical labels, coarse-label correction, and network optimization in two separately analyzed Darcy/Fourier neural operator studies. An initial training-dominated experiment does not establish independent benefit for a last-layer gradient acquisition proxy. A prospective comparison uses multigrid-preconditioned conjugate gradients, 21 equally budgeted tuning trials, and 35 formal trajectories across five independent training pools and optimization seeds, with dependent 120- and 180-second checkpoints. At the 180-second cap, reusing 128 high-fidelity labels attains mean ID error of '''+f"{pct('hf128'):.3f}\\%, compared with {pct('hf_online'):.3f}\\% for online high-fidelity acquisition and {pct('phys32'):.3f}\\% for corrected labels on a 32-interval coarse space. The latter also exceeds the {pct('coarse32'):.3f}\\% error of uncorrected coarse supervision in all five pairs. Correction nevertheless strongly improves the eight-interval coarse baseline. "+r'''Numerical screening independently shows substantial one-cycle correction bias when the coarse space is inadequate. These results expose sensitivity to the numerical solver, reuse-set size, and available optimization time; they do not establish a universally optimal allocation rule or cross-PDE superiority.
\end{abstract}'''
m=m[:a]+abstract+m[b:]
m=m.replace('The current evidence supports a reproducible runtime ledger with explicit exclusions, a controlled negative result concerning one last-layer acquisition proxy, and an examination of correction bias across mesh ratios and contrast. A validated allocation rule across tasks remains a prospective objective.','The current evidence combines a runtime ledger with explicit exclusions, a negative result concerning one last-layer acquisition proxy, correction-bias screening, and a prospective replicated comparison against efficient HF solves and fixed-HF-label reuse. Stronger baselines change the interpretation: a correction method can outperform a 32-label reuse comparator while losing to a 128-label comparator. A validated allocation rule across tasks remains a prospective objective.')
m=m.replace('\\input{formal_results.tex}','\\input{formal_results.tex}\n\\input{formal_interpretation.tex}')
a=m.index('\\section{Discussion and limitations}');b=m.index('\\section{Conclusion}',a)
m=m[:a]+r'''\section{Discussion and limitations}
Allocation depends jointly on correction bias, cache saturation, and solve-to-update cost. The original experiment is training-dominated and nearly exhausts its label pool. The new experiment uses a stronger HF solver, an 8192-input pool, independent training pools, and fixed-label reuse comparators. Its strongest tested mean configuration uses 128 accurate labels repeatedly. The poor 32-label result and the mixed correction results rule out the simple prescriptions ``always buy more labels'', ``always train longer'', or ``always correct coarse labels'' within the tested menu.

These are selected-configuration comparisons, not isolated causal estimates of correction or acquisition. The tuning horizon is shorter than the formal horizon, and only one tuning pool is used. Five training units cannot support precise small-effect claims; two dependent checkpoints cannot identify scaling exponents. Shared evaluation inputs do not add independent training replicates. The short-budget results cannot be pooled with the earlier twenty-minute study or the single-seed pilot.

One PDE family and one FNO do not establish universality. Restricting refined numerical solutions to a fixed output grid defines a projected-output problem, not full fine-grid prediction. CPU timing does not transfer automatically to GPU execution; the appendix contains only portability microbenchmarks. The multigrid baseline is a validated local solver implementation, not a claim of numerical optimality. A second architecture and held-out numerical regime are needed before extending the allocation conclusions. Any target-error allocation rule would need prospective evaluation on settings not used to formulate it.

'''+m[b:]
a=m.index('\\section{Conclusion}');b=m.index('\\section*{Reproducibility and declarations}',a)
m=m[:a]+r'''\section{Conclusion}
The tested gradient proxy does not establish independent benefit in the original training-dominated regime. In the prospective strong-solver experiment, reusing 128 HF labels gives the lowest mean error among the tested configurations, while 32 labels are insufficient to match online acquisition. One-cycle correction helps a very coarse target but fails to improve the selected 32-interval coarse baseline under the same construction cap. These unfavorable outcomes remain central evidence. Measured budgets and strong fixed-label reuse baselines are necessary to assess multifidelity value; broader allocation laws remain unvalidated.

'''+m[b:]
m=m.replace('\\appendix','\\clearpage\n\\appendix')
(paper/'main.tex').write_text(m)
